Z||000006||West Coast Fiero||10-22-2001||01:29 PM||||honestly, the best " backyeard " way to check if the mounts are done is to put the car in gear or park, raise the rear decklid, and grab a hold of the engine and try to move it fore and aft. If there is any great movement with little resistance then the dogbone is kapput, the second item to go after the dog bone is most commonly the front transmission mount, then the rear, then the carrier bearings and/or the CV joints. The front engine mount does go out, but not as often as the others as it is not exposed to torsional loads as it is mounted center to the crank, however oil and heat, like the other mounts, can deteriorate the mount and cause it to fail, especially 88 front engine mounts which are oil-filled.<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
